  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 216 PM CST Wed Nov 4 2026\n\nElongated surface high pressure over south central Canada into the \neastern Rockies will gradually slide eastward this afternoon \nthrough Friday. This will result in winds becoming light and \nvariable in western and north central North Dakota tonight. \nModels indicate that cloud cover is favored over the eastern \nhalf of the state as it remains somewhat under the influence of \nlow pressure sliding eastward towards the Great Lakes Region. \nThis includes low-level stratus that may extend from the James \nRiver Valley up through the Turtle Mountains. The western half \nis less certain with some models producing high clouds over \nparts of the west. Air aloft remains relatively dry though, so \nwouldn't be surprised if this isn't a little overdone. For areas\nwhere skies clear off/remain clear and winds become light and \nvariable, dense fog development is possible. Therefore, added \nareas of fog mainly west of Highway 83 and patchy fog along and \neast of Highway 83.\n\nBeyond potential fog tonight, the entire forecast is very \nbenign with the NBM producing one big weather grid, or in other \nwords, no mentionable precipitation chances. As aforementioned \nsurface high slides through, high temperatures Thursday will be \nthe coldest for the remainder of the week as cooler Canadian air\nfilters into much of the state. This will result in highs \nranging from the upper 30s east to low 50s southwest. Multiple \ntransitory ridges Thursday night through the middle of next \nweek, along with surface flow frequently taking on a westerly \ncomponent, will help maintain above average temperatures through\nthe middle of next week. By Saturday, this translates to highs \nmostly in the 50s with some 60 degree readings, especially in \nthe southwest and south central. While these aren't particularly\nunusual temperatures for this time of year with average highs \nin the mid 40s and records still reaching the mid to upper 70s; \nthey will become more and more above average as the calendar \nadvances further into November.",
